No. pg_class holds only relation-like objects. Triggers and functions have their own catalogs.

What pg_class contains (distinguished by relkind):

relkind	Object
r	ordinary table
i	index
S	sequence
t	TOAST table
v	view
m	materialized view
c	composite type
f	foreign table
p	partitioned table
I	partitioned index

Where the others live

Object	Catalog	Useful view
Functions / procedures / aggregates	pg_proc	information_schema.routines
Triggers	pg_trigger (tgrelid links to pg_class.oid)	information_schema.triggers
Constraints	pg_constraint (conrelid links to pg_class.oid)	information_schema.table_constraints
Columns	pg_attribute (attrelid links to pg_class.oid)	information_schema.columns
Types	pg_type	
Schemas	pg_namespace	
Index details	pg_index (indexrelid / indrelid link to pg_class.oid)	pg_indexes
Dependencies	pg_depend

How they relate: a trigger is attached to a table, so pg_trigger.tgrelid points to pg_class.oid, and pg_trigger.tgfoid points to the trigger function in pg_proc.oid. A function is a standalone object and is not in pg_class at all.

Example queries (triggers on a table, with their functions):

sql
select c.relnamespace::regnamespace as schema,
       c.relname as table_name,
       t.tgname as trigger_name,
       p.proname as function_name,
       pg_get_triggerdef(t.oid) as definition
from pg_trigger t
join pg_class c on c.oid = t.tgrelid
join pg_proc  p on p.oid = t.tgfoid
where not t.tgisinternal
order by 1, 2, 3;

not t.tgisinternal filters out the internal triggers PostgreSQL creates for foreign keys.
